      "description": "A “Pirates &amp; Highwaymen” cigarette card published by the W.D. &amp; H.O. Wills company in 1925, number 15 of a series of 25,  titled “Sir Henry Morgan,—2. The Capture of Panama.” The reverse description reads: “In 1670, Morgan, then Commander-in-Chief of all the ships of war of Jamaica, resolved to undertake the capture of Panama. His small force of some 1,200 men crossed the isthmus, through tropical jungle, enduring terrible hardships from hunger on the journey. The Spanish President of Panama had about 3,000 infantry, cavalry, and artillery drawn up before the city, besides a great herd of wild bulls intended to break the ranks of the British. The Spaniards were utterly routed, and the rich city was burned and plundered. In Feb. 1671, Morgan and his buccaneers left Panama with their booty; 175 beasts of burden laden with treasure, and about 600 slaves.” Cherrygarden collection; scanned by Fred Cherrygarden.",
